= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Order configurations coordinatewise: $\omega\le\eta$ means $\omega(e)\le\eta(e)$ for every <edge>. Then <stochastic domination> is the condition
$$
\boxed{\mu_1(A)\ge\mu_2(A)\quad\text{for every increasing event }A.}
$$
Equivalently, $\mathbb E_{\mu_1}f\ge\mathbb E_{\mu_2}f$ for every real-valued <order-preserving function> $f$. The equivalence follows on this finite space by writing $f$, up to an additive constant, as a nonnegative linear combination of indicators of its increasing upper level sets. A <coupling of probability distributions> supported on pairs $(X_1,X_2)$ with $X_1\ge X_2$ proves this domination; conversely such an ordered <coupling of probability distributions> exists on a finite partially ordered space.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the coordinatewise lattice operations $\omega\vee\eta$ and $\omega\wedge\eta$, <Holley's condition> is
$$
\boxed{\mu_1(\omega\vee\eta)\mu_2(\omega\wedge\eta)
\ge\mu_1(\omega)\mu_2(\eta)\quad\text{for all }\omega,\eta.}
$$
This sufficient condition implies $\mu_1\ge\mu_2$ by the <Holley inequality>. It is often stated first for strictly positive <probability> weights, as will hold for the random-cluster application, but the displayed condition is also sufficient for nonnegative weights.

For clarity about the zero-weight case, apply the <four functions theorem> to an <increasing event> $A$, with the first two functions $\mu_1\mathbf1_{A^c}$ and $\mu_2\mathbf1_A$, and the last two $\mu_1\mathbf1_A$ and $\mu_2\mathbf1_{A^c}$. If $\omega\notin A$ and $\eta\in A$, then $\omega\vee\eta\in A$ and $\omega\wedge\eta\notin A$. The pointwise hypothesis is exactly the displayed cross-lattice inequality. Its summed conclusion gives
$$
\mu_1(A^c)\mu_2(A)\le\mu_1(A)\mu_2(A^c),
$$
which rearranges to $\mu_1(A)\ge\mu_2(A)$. This establishes the same statement without a positivity-of-support assumption.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Identify a configuration with its set $S$ of open <edges>. Let $k(S)$ count the <connected components of a graph> on the full <graph vertex> set, including isolated <graph vertices>. The <random-cluster measure> is
$$
\mu_{p,q}(S)=\frac1{Z_{p,q}}p^{|S|}(1-p)^{|E|-|S|}q^{k(S)}.
$$
For $p\in(0,1)$ and finite $q\ge1$ all its weights are positive. We compare $1\le q_1\le q_2$, taking $\mu_1=\mu_{p,q_1}$ and $\mu_2=\mu_{p,q_2}$.

The key fact is <supermodularity of graph component count>:
$$
k(S\cap T)+k(S\cup T)\ge k(S)+k(T).
$$
To prove it, orient the graph <edges> arbitrarily and let $U_S$ be the real span of their incidence vectors for <edges> in $S$. Its <dimension of a vector space> is $r(S)=|V|-k(S)$: on each <connected component of a graph> these vectors span the vectors whose coordinates sum to zero. Since $U_{S\cup T}=U_S+U_T$ and $U_{S\cap T}\subset U_S\cap U_T$, the <dimension formula for a sum of subspaces> gives
$$
r(S\cup T)+r(S\cap T)\le r(S)+r(T),
$$
which is exactly the component-count inequality.

Put $a=k(S)-k(S\cup T)\ge0$ and $b=k(S\cap T)-k(T)\ge0$. The preceding inequality gives $b\ge a$. In the ratio of the two sides of <Holley's condition>, the normalization constants and Bernoulli <edge> factors cancel, because $|S\cup T|+|S\cap T|=|S|+|T|$. The ratio is therefore
$$
\frac{\mu_1(S\cup T)\mu_2(S\cap T)}{\mu_1(S)\mu_2(T)}
=q_1^{-a}q_2^b
=\left(\frac{q_2}{q_1}\right)^a q_2^{b-a}\ge1.
$$
By the <Holley inequality>, $\mu_{p,q_1}$ <stochastically dominates> $\mu_{p,q_2}$. Thus **increasing <cluster weight> suppresses every <increasing event>**:
$$
\boxed{\mu_{p,q_1}(A)\ge\mu_{p,q_2}(A)\quad(q_1\le q_2).}
$$
The <random-cluster single-edge conditional probability> confirms the mechanism: it is $p$ when the endpoints are already connected, and $p/(p+q(1-p))$ otherwise. Larger $q$ favours configurations with more separate components. The condition $q\ge1$ enters explicitly in the last factor $q_2^{b-a}$.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use consistently wired boundary conditions in increasing finite boxes and let $\phi_{p,q}^{\mathrm w}$ be the usual wired infinite-volume <random-cluster measure>. The finite-volume comparison just proved applies with boundary <graph vertices> identified, since it is still a comparison on a finite graph. Passing to the infinite-volume limit preserves the inequality for every <increasing event> determined by finitely many <edges>.

For fixed $m$, let $A_m$ be the event that the origin connects by an <open path> in $[-m,m]^2$ to its boundary. It is such a finite <increasing event>. Therefore, for $q_1\le q_2$,
$$
\phi_{p,q_1}^{\mathrm w}(A_m)\ge\phi_{p,q_2}^{\mathrm w}(A_m).
$$
The events decrease to the event that the origin has an <infinite percolation cluster>, by local finiteness. <Continuity from above of a measure> gives
$$
\theta^{\mathrm w}(p,q_1)\ge\theta^{\mathrm w}(p,q_2).
$$
Define the <random-cluster critical probability> by $p_c(q)=\inf\{p:\theta^{\mathrm w}(p,q)>0\}$. The set in which percolation occurs at $q_2$ is a subset of that at $q_1$, whence
$$
\boxed{q_1\le q_2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad p_c(q_1)\le p_c(q_2).}
$$
The same proof works if free boundary conditions are used consistently throughout. It compares critical <probabilities>; it does not presume whether an <infinite percolation cluster> exists at the critical parameter itself.

= 4
{parent=Paper 30}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <Burton-Keane theorem> says that for independent <bond percolation> on the <hypercubic lattice> $\mathbb Z^d$, $d\ge2$, at every fixed $p\in[0,1]$, there is almost surely **at most one <infinite percolation cluster>**. It does not assert that such a cluster exists, nor does it assert absence of percolation at the critical parameter. The cases $p=0$ and $p=1$ are immediate; assume $0<p<1$ for the proof.

Let $N_\infty$ be the number of <infinite percolation clusters>. This <random variable> is translation invariant. By <translation ergodicity of Bernoulli percolation>, it is almost surely a constant $k\in\{0,1,2,\ldots,\infty\}$. One way to see ergodicity is to approximate an invariant event by events on finite sets of <edges>, translate one approximation far enough to make the two approximations independent, and then let the approximation error vanish. Its <probability> equals its square, hence is zero or one.

The other probabilistic input is the <finite-energy property of Bernoulli percolation>. Conditionally on all <edges> outside a finite set, every prescribed inside configuration has positive <probability>, namely a product of powers of $p$ and $1-p$. Thus an event of positive <probability> can be followed by a specified finite modification and still gives a positive-probability image event. Outside infinite arms are unaffected.

First exclude a finite $k\ge2$. Some finite box meets at least two <infinite percolation clusters> with positive <probability>. Open all <edges> in and incident to that box. These clusters merge. Opening finitely many <edges> cannot create an <infinite percolation cluster> solely out of finite clusters, since it joins only finitely many such clusters. Hence the number of <infinite percolation clusters> decreases with positive <probability>, contradicting its almost-sure constant value. This rules out every finite multiplicity greater than one.

Now suppose $k=\infty$. With positive <probability> a finite box meets at least three distinct <infinite percolation clusters>. Retain an exterior infinite ray from each of three of them. Replace the finitely many interior and boundary <edges> by a minimal <tree> joining their three attachment <graph vertices>, and close all unused <edges> there. The exterior rays belong to distinct original clusters, so have no outside connections to each other. The resulting joined cluster has a branch <graph vertex> $v$ with exactly three incident open <edges>, and deleting $v$ leaves three infinite components. If a terminal lies in the middle of the interior connecting path, its exterior ray supplies the third branch there. Such a <graph vertex> is a <trifurcation vertex in percolation>. A finite box has only finitely many candidate locations and modifications; therefore some fixed <graph vertex> has positive trifurcation <probability>. By translation invariance, that <probability> is a common number $\tau>0$ at every <graph vertex>.

The decisive deterministic input is the <trifurcation boundary-counting lemma>. Let $T$ be all these degree-three <trifurcation vertices in percolation> in a finite box $B_n$. In each <infinite percolation cluster> meeting $T$, delete the <graph vertices> of $T$, contract each remaining <connected component of a graph> adjacent to $T$ to a node, and retain the incidences with the deleted <graph vertices>, including direct <edges> between <graph vertices> of $T$. This is a finite <tree>: a cycle would connect two neighbours of an original <trifurcation vertex in percolation> without passing through it, contradicting its three-way separation. Every deleted <graph vertex> has degree three. Every leaf component is infinite, since a finite leaf would give a finite branch after removing its adjacent <trifurcation vertex in percolation>. The elementary <tree> degree identity therefore supplies at least $|T\cap C|+2$ infinite leaf components for a cluster containing $T$.

Each such infinite component reaches the outer <graph vertex> boundary $\partial^+B_n$, and different components reach different boundary <graph vertices>. Summing over the clusters yields, in particular,
$$
|T|\le|\partial^+B_n|.
$$
For a nearest-neighbour lattice box,
$$
|B_n|=(2n+1)^d,\qquad
|\partial^+B_n|=2d(2n+1)^{d-1}.
$$
Taking expectations and using the common trifurcation <probability> gives
$$
\tau(2n+1)^d\le2d(2n+1)^{d-1},\qquad
\tau\le\frac{2d}{2n+1}\longrightarrow0.
$$
This contradicts $\tau>0$ and excludes infinitely many <infinite percolation clusters>. Thus only $k=0$ or $k=1$ remains:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P_p(N_\infty\in\{0,1\})=1\quad\text{for every }p\in[0,1].}
$$
The proof explains the role of the <amenable boundary growth of lattice boxes>: <finite-energy property of Bernoulli percolation> would make branching points have positive volume density, while their disjoint infinite branches can only be supplied through a boundary of lower order. It uses no planar duality and works in every indicated dimension.
